## Engineering Principles — REQUIRED

All implementations MUST follow:

### DRY (Don’t Repeat Yourself)
- No duplicated logic
- Extract reusable utilities/components/hooks
- Centralize shared behavior

### KISS (Keep It Simple)
- Prefer simple solutions over complex abstractions
- Avoid over-engineering
- Build only what is needed

---

## Architecture Principles (MANDATORY)

### 1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)
- Each component/module has ONE responsibility
- Split large components into smaller focused ones

### 2. Open/Closed Principle (OCP)
- Extend behavior without modifying existing code
- Prefer composition, config, or props over mutation

### 3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)
- Components must be replaceable without breaking behavior
- Maintain consistent interfaces/contracts

### 4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)
- Avoid large, bloated interfaces/props
- Prefer small, focused APIs

### 5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)
- Depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations
- Use hooks, context, or injected dependencies when appropriate

---

## Composition Rules

### ✅ Composition OVER Inheritance
- Prefer reusable components/hooks over class hierarchies
- Build complex features by combining simple parts

---

## Coupling & Access Rules

### Law of Demeter (LoD)
- Components/modules only talk to direct dependencies
- No deep chaining (e.g. `a.b.c.d`)
- Use intermediaries (hooks, selectors, helpers)

---

## Frontend Implementation Standards

### Component Design
- Small, reusable, and focused components
- No “god components”
- Co-locate logic when appropriate

### State Management
- Keep state minimal and localized
- Lift state only when necessary
- Avoid unnecessary global state

### Styling (Tailwind)
- Use Tailwind utility-first approach
- Avoid inline styles unless necessary
- Keep class usage consistent and readable

### Animation (motion.dev)
- Use motion.dev for animations ONLY
- Keep animations purposeful (not decorative noise)

---

## File & Code Organization

- Group by feature, not by type
- Keep folders shallow and readable
- Avoid unnecessary nesting

---

## Anti-Patterns (STRICTLY AVOID)

❌ Over-engineering simple features  
❌ Large monolithic components  
❌ Duplicate logic across files  
❌ Introducing new dependencies without approval  
❌ Deep prop drilling chains  
❌ Tight coupling between modules  
❌ Premature optimization  

---

## Subagent Expectations

When generating specs or code, subagents MUST:

- Follow ALL principles above
- Justify any deviation explicitly
- Prefer simpler implementations first
- Avoid adding dependencies unless approved
- Produce modular, maintainable code

If a tradeoff is made:
→ It MUST be documented in the spec

